<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Opium War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Second Anglo-Chinese War, Arrow War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Opium War was fought by the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> between 1856 and 1860. It began after Qing officials seized the British-registered ship Arrow in October 1856 and British gunboats shelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>; the allies stormed Canton in December 1857, took the Taku Forts in 1858 and forced the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tientsin</a>. When the Xianfeng Emperor refused to ratify it, fighting resumed, and after hostages were tortured or murdered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce-8th-earl-of-elgin">Lord Elgin</a> had the Old Summer Palace looted and burned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a> signed the Convention of Peking in October 1860, which forced legalisation of the opium trade and ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a> Peninsula to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a>, which ended with the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanking</a> of 1842: an indemnity and extraterritoriality for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, five treaty ports and the cession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a>. The treaty did not satisfy British hopes of better trade and diplomatic relations. In the 1850s Britain, citing its most favoured nation status, demanded that the treaty be renegotiated.</p><p>The Arrow, a cargo ship once used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, had been registered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> as British, though its registration had expired. When Chinese marines seized it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> on suspicion of piracy and arrested most of its crew, the consul Harry Parkes demanded their release and an apology for the alleged insult to the flag. The viceroy Ye Mingchen released nine of the twelve arrested and refused the other three.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Under Admiral Sir Michael Seymour the British and French took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>, a city of more than a million people, with fewer than 6,000 troops. They then sailed north and took the Taku Forts in May 1858, after which the Treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tientsin</a> were signed in June with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, opening eleven more ports and allowing foreign legations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>.</p><p>In June 1859 the guns of the Mongol general Sengge Rinchen sank four British gunboats at the forts, a blow to British prestige. In 1860 an Anglo-French force of 11,000 British and 6,700 French troops landed near Beitang and took the forts on 21 August. Parkes and other prisoners were then seized and some were tortured and killed. The emperor fled, and on 18 October British soldiers burned the Old Summer Palace, the French declining to take part.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a> signed the Convention of Peking on 24 October 1860. It confirmed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tientsin</a>, opened Tianjin as a trade port, legalised the opium trade, granted Christians civil rights including freedom to evangelise, and ceded part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Russia, through Ignatiev, also obtained a permanent presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and, in a supplementary treaty, the territory east of the Ussuri, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a> was founded.</p><p>Many Chinese officials came to see conflicts with Western powers as part of a looming national crisis rather than traditional wars. A modernisation effort, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a>, followed, and the Qing government was able to concentrate on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a>. In Britain William Ewart Gladstone called the opium trade most infamous and atrocious.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Opium War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Second Opium War</b> (1856–1860) British and French forces took the Dagu forts and marched on Beijing, and in October 1860 their troops looted the Yuanmingyuan, the Old Summer Palace, which the British then burned, while the Xianfeng Emperor fled to Rehe beyond the Great Wall.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Foreign troops occupied the palace twice: Anglo-French forces in 1860 during the <b>Second Opium War</b>, and the forces of the treaty powers in 1900 and the following year, after Empress Dowager Cixi fled during the Boxer Rebellion.</blockquote><a href="/work/forbidden-city">Forbidden City · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>When China opened its borders to foreign merchants and visitors after its defeat in the First and <b>Second Opium Wars</b>, the wall became a main attraction for tourists, and the travelogues of the later 19th century enhanced its reputation and its mythology.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall of China · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It served on the Yangtze Patrol, begun after the <b>Second Opium War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-12">12 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain used the incident as grounds for the <b>Second Opium War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-8">8 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
